{
 "id": "T05-pinned-pegs",
 "title": "Jaw centreline offset at the fingertips vs roll axis (X, closing axis)",
 "mode": "linear",
 "units": "mm (pinch point x vs roll axis, gripper frame G)",
 "requirement": "Pinch point within +/-0.5 mm of its calibrated position. The systematic shift from finger B lagging by half the design backlash (the CAD mesh is centred, so B lags the CAD theta = 0 by jt/(2r); the centre moves half of that: 0.25 * jt0 * L_tip / r) is calibrated into the TCP, so the spec is centred on it.",
 "notes": "Kinematics. The jaw centre is x_c = (x_A + x_B)/2 + finger terms. Base layout: pivot A is the servo shaft (x_A = cradle + float + shaft + spline + knuckle A on horn) and pin B is a hole in the housing (x_B = pin B hole + pin float), so every term enters at 0.5. Slotted idler (review fix): pin B is set against gear A at assembly, so x_B = x_A + a and x_c = x_A + (a - a0)/2 with a from T06-slotted (same shared names). The servo chain then enters at 1.0, pinB_pos_to_B drops out, and slot setting and pin float enter at 0.5. spline_play and knuckleA_on_horn enter x_A at +1 and a at -1 (T06-slotted), so they net +0.5. Pinned layout (fix 9): x_A = pin A hole to datum B + knuckle A float; a from T06-bushed, so pin A float nets +0.5 and the hole-to-hole term and pin B float enter at 0.5. The dog coupling's lash rotates A and (through the 1:1 mesh) B by equal and opposite angles, so the tips move symmetrically and the centre does not move (sensitivity 0, carried so the loop is complete; it matters for T09-pinned).",
